      <section id="position-sizing" aria-labelledby="position-sizing-heading">
        <p class="article-kicker">05</p>
        <h2 id="position-sizing-heading">Position sizing</h2>
        <p>
          During this period, I risked roughly 20% to 30% of my account when margin allowed. On a
          $10,000 account, that means accepting a possible $2,000 to $3,000 loss on one trade. At
          the document's 5:1 risk ratio, the target profit would be $400 to $600.
        </p>
        <aside class="article-note">
          <p>
            This is a disclosure, not a sizing recommendation. Decide the maximum account loss you
            can accept before entering any values in the calculator.
          </p>
        </aside>
        <p>
          The calculator takes account size, all three limit prices, the first take-profit level,
          and the stop. It returns the three quantities, adjusted take-profit levels, and potential
          profit for each fill scenario.
        </p>
        <p>
          Its default calculation uses 10% account risk. The original method changes that input by
          entering twice the account size for 20% risk, half the account size for 5% risk, and so
          on. Verify every output against your exchange's contract size and margin rules before
          placing an order.
        </p>
        <p>
          <a
            href="https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/16-xoM2-JJkOANUb67w9ADrl8J-ZGC7qt7pl5h1fg93I/edit?gid=0#gid=0"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noreferrer"
          >Crypto DCA trend continuation strategy calculator ↗</a>
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="risking-five" aria-labelledby="risking-five-heading">
        <p class="article-kicker">06</p>
        <h2 id="risking-five-heading">Risking five to make one</h2>
        <p>
          The third-fill version risks $5 for a $1 target. The method trades a large potential loss
          for a wider stop and a closer exit. Its operating target is six winning trades out of
          seven.
        </p>
        <p>
          The appeal is practical. A wide stop tolerates more market noise, while the closer target
          asks less of each move. Fewer tight stop-outs may also reduce emotional re-entry and
          repeated transaction costs.
        </p>
        <p>
          A wide stop does not create an edge by itself. The approach depends on selective entries,
          a very high realized win rate, consistent sizing, and the discipline to accept the full
          loss when the setup fails.
        </p>
        <ul class="article-list">
          <li>Judge performance across a series of trades, not one result.</li>
          <li>Do not tighten the shared stop after deeper orders fill.</li>
          <li>Do not add a fourth order when the planned setup is invalidated.</li>
          <li>Track whether the live win rate supports the loss size.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section id="win-rate-checklist" aria-labelledby="win-rate-checklist-heading">
        <p class="article-kicker">07</p>
        <h2 id="win-rate-checklist-heading">My checklist for cleaner setups</h2>

        <h3>Market conditions</h3>
        <ul class="article-list">
          <li>Prefer orderly price action with a readable Elliott-wave structure.</li>
          <li>Avoid extreme volatility that can run through every planned level.</li>
          <li>Avoid FOMC decisions, US elections, CPI releases, war, and geopolitical shocks.</li>
          <li>Avoid the New York session open between 8:00 and 10:00 a.m. Eastern.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Timeframes and bias</h3>
        <ul class="article-list">
          <li>Use the 3-minute chart for the entry setup.</li>
          <li>Check the daily chart first for trend direction and major levels.</li>
          <li>Choose a long or short daily bias before scanning entries.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Levels and review</h3>
        <ul class="article-list">
          <li>Mark the previous day's high and low, weekly levels, and round numbers.</li>
          <li>Watch how price breaks or rejects those areas.</li>
          <li>Backtest the complete rules on historical charts.</li>
          <li>Record entries, fills, exits, invalidations, and the running win rate.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          The best setups often appear in uneventful conditions. Waiting is part of the strategy.
        </p>
      </section>

